# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-cad-launch-new-by-default -- launch a new CAD by default; attach only on request, optionally to a given instance
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: feature
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: cad-launch-new-by-default
#+DATE: 2026-10-23

* Request

pjb, 2026-10-23, following alfe-cad-quit-saves-modified-drawing:

#+begin_quote
Interactively, the user may want to connect to a launched instance. But in
batch (eg CI), we want to launch a new instance. And given the mutex
constraints, in batch we don't want another instance running at the same
time. So for alfe, the default should be to launch a new instance, and we
should add an explicit option to connect to an existing instance (and there
must be a way to differentiate running instances, and to specify which one
we want to connect to, cf the COM stuff, and on unix I guess the PID would
be used). [...] For alfe, by default, launch a new instance (even with COM or
AppleScript), unless the user give an option indicating to connect to an
existing instance or a specific existing instance. [...] for unix we don't
have a CAD running on linux to test [...] so ignore this option.
#+end_quote

Cleaning up leftover CADs is the CI JOB SCRIPTS' business, not alfe's.

* State before

- The Windows COM bridges (AutoCAD always in automation mode, BricsCAD
  with --mode automation) ran in COM mode "auto": GetObject(, ProgID)
  first, CreateObject only when nothing was running. AUTOCAD_COM_MODE /
  BRICSCAD_COM_MODE could select "attach" or "create"; nothing on the
  command line did.
- --backend {attach,launch} was in the usage text and parsed into
  CLI-OPTIONS-BACKEND-VARIANT, but only the clautolisp backend read that
  slot: the CAD backends ignored it.
- macOS BricsCAD batch mode (the default) already starts the executable
  itself -- a new process every run. The opt-in AppleScript path uses
  `open -a', which activates a running copy, and finds the app by process
  NAME, so with two copies it cannot tell them apart.
- Two consequences once a second instance can exist:
  - quit-created-autocad's VBScript re-attaches with GetObject(, ProgID),
    which may name an instance this run did not create;
  - RecordCreatedProcesses records EVERY acad.exe started with
    /Automation, not just the one this run started.

* Measure first

Probe scripts/probe-cad-instances.{ps1,sh}, family cad-instances. It
measures:
- whether a second CreateObject / launch gives a second process;
- which instance GetActiveObject returns;
- what the ROT holds per instance (class moniker, file monikers of saved
  documents);
- whether binding a document's file moniker reaches its instance;
- whether System Events can make one of two same-named BricsCAD processes
  frontmost by unix id.

* Measured (MR !475, 2026-10-23)

| question                          | AutoCAD 2022 (job 17419746926)    | BricsCAD V25 Windows (17419746925) | BricsCAD V26 macOS (17419256134) |
|-----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+------------------------------------+----------------------------------|
| second create/launch: new process | yes (49752, 40648)                | yes (41632, 27996)                 | yes, direct and =open -n=        |
| GetActiveObject, two running      | the FIRST, always                 | the FIRST, always                  | (=open -a= activates the oldest) |
| ROT per instance                  | class monikers twice, both -> 1st | the same                           | --                               |
| saved drawing in the ROT          | no                                | no                                 | --                               |
| target one instance by PID        | only if it is the first           | only if it is the first            | yes: System Events, unix id      |

Binding a .dwg path the ROT does not hold makes COM start or wait on a CAD
for it: the first BricsCAD run hung 20 minutes there (cancelled; the probe
now binds only real ROT entries and kills every CAD process it started).

The second macOS run (17419746924) found a leftover BricsCAD (48160,
SCHME profile, MNL error) running before it, and pjb at the machine held
the focus during FRONT-BY-PID; the first run, unattended, is the reference.

* Done (2.3.45)

- =--backend launch= (default for CADs) / =--backend attach=, now honoured
  by the CAD backends; =--attach-pid PID= (implies attach). An attach
  implies =--mode automation=; with =--mode batch= it is a usage error
  (APPLY-CAD-INSTANCE-POLICY).
- Windows COM bridges: mode "launch" (CreateObject only) by default
  (CAD-COM-MODE; =$AUTOCAD_COM_MODE= / =$BRICSCAD_COM_MODE= still choose
  when no --backend is given). =--attach-pid=: CHECK-COM-ATTACH-PID asks
  PowerShell for the PID behind GetActiveObject and refuses any other PID,
  naming the reachable one.
- AutoCAD bridge: records only the acad.exe its CreateObject started (PIDs
  before vs after), as CREATEDPID= in the flags file; at shutdown, with
  another acad.exe running, ends its own by PID (TASKKILL, /F after the
  timeout) instead of the quit bridge's GetObject.
- macOS AppleScript path: launch = =open -n -a= and the PID that appeared;
  attach = =open -a='s instance, or the =--attach-pid= one; every step then
  addresses that one process by unix id. An explicit attach counts as the
  macOS automation opt-in.
- Not alfe's: killing leftover CADs before a CI job (job scripts; SCHMS /
  SCHME handed to another agent).
- Not verified live on a CAD: --backend attach / --attach-pid end to end
  (no CI job attaches, by design). The launch default is exercised by every
  CAD job of the pipeline.
